 /**
- * Foreground service (type connectedDevice) that owns the link server and the mDNS advert. Its
- * foreground state is what keeps the launcher's network open behind other apps and in Doze.
+ * The link's home in the launcher's process: the link server, the mDNS advert and the unlock
+ * receiver run for as long as the process lives ([ensureLink], idempotent), whichever way it was
+ * started: the home screen ([app.lawnchair.LawnchairApp]), a boot or an update, a crash-restart
+ * alarm ([BootReceiver]).
  *
- * It shares the launcher's process, so nothing may throw out of its lifecycle: when Android
- * refuses the foreground start (e.g. ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException for a sticky
- * restart while another app is in front), the service logs it and stops itself; the next start
- * (home screen shown, boot, update, crash alarm) tries again.
+ * No foreground service and no notification (the owner, 2026-10-17: nothing stays in the shade;
+ * notifications only when he has to do something). What keeps the link reachable instead:
+ * - Homa Lawn is the default home app, so Android keeps its process at home-app priority while
+ *   another app is in front: not cached, never frozen;
+ * - "Keep connected" (the battery-optimisation exemption, asked by [LinkAsks]): Android lets an
+ *   exempt app use the network in Doze, and does not stop this service when the app goes idle.
  *
- * Its notification is also the phone's view of the link: how many computers are paired, which
- * one is connected right now, whether screen control was used in the last [SCREEN_SHOWN_MS],
- * and "Pair a computer", which opens Lawnchair's pairing screen ([PairConfirmActivity]); the
